---
import CodeBlock from './CodeBlock.astro'
import QuickStart from './QuickStart.astro'

interface Props {
  framework?: 'react' | 'vue' | 'svelte'
}
const { framework = 'react' } = Astro.props
const guide = {
  react: {
    cli: 'shadcn@latest',
    cliName: 'shadcn',
    project: 'React',
    compatible: 'shadcn-compatible React',
    registryPath: '/r/text-bloom.json',
    createCommand: 'npx shadcn@latest init --template vite --name my-app --no-monorepo',
    createNote:
      'Choose a component library and preset when prompted, enter the generated directory, then continue with the installation command below.',
    setupUrl: 'https://ui.shadcn.com/docs/installation',
    setupName: 'shadcn framework setup',
    packageName: '@kida-ui/react',
    directory: 'src/components/ui/kida',
    importPath: '@/components/ui/kida/text-bloom',
  },
  vue: {
    cli: 'shadcn-vue@2.8.2',
    cliName: 'shadcn-vue',
    project: 'Vue 3.5+ TypeScript',
    compatible: 'shadcn-vue-compatible Vue',
    registryPath: '/r/vue/text-bloom.json',
    createCommand: 'pnpm create vite my-app --template vue-ts',
    createNote:
      'Enter the new project, install dependencies, and complete the Vue Vite setup linked below (including the @ alias) before initializing shadcn-vue.',
    setupUrl: 'https://www.shadcn-vue.com/docs/installation/vite',
    setupName: 'shadcn-vue Vite setup',
    packageName: '@kida-ui/vue',
    directory: 'src/components/ui/kida',
    importPath: '@/components/ui/kida/text-bloom.vue',
  },
  svelte: {
    cli: 'shadcn-svelte@1.8.0',
    cliName: 'shadcn-svelte',
    project: 'Svelte 5 TypeScript (SvelteKit or Vite)',
    compatible: 'shadcn-svelte-compatible Svelte',
    registryPath: '/r/svelte/text-bloom.json',
    createCommand: 'npx sv create my-app --template minimal --types ts --add tailwindcss',
    createNote:
      'Enter the new project and install dependencies. shadcn-svelte requires Tailwind CSS in the project, so the command above adds it; Kida components do not depend on it.',
    setupUrl: 'https://shadcn-svelte.com/docs/installation',
    setupName: 'shadcn-svelte setup',
    packageName: '@kida-ui/svelte',
    directory: 'src/lib/components/ui/kida',
    importPath: '$lib/components/ui/kida/text-bloom.svelte',
  },
}[framework]
const registryUrl = new URL(guide.registryPath, Astro.site).href
const installCommand = `npx ${guide.cli} add ${registryUrl}`
const initCommand = `npx ${guide.cli} init`
const structure = {
  react: `src/components/ui/kida/
├── text-bloom.tsx
├── text-bloom.css
├── compose-refs.ts
├── use-isomorphic-layout-effect.ts
└── motion/
    ├── text-bloom.ts
    └── reduced-motion.ts`,
  vue: `src/components/ui/kida/
├── text-bloom.vue
├── text-bloom.types.ts
├── text-bloom.css
├── text-bloom.motion.ts
└── reduced-motion.motion.ts`,
  svelte: `src/lib/components/ui/kida/
├── text-bloom.svelte
├── text-bloom.types.ts
├── text-bloom.css
├── text-bloom.motion.ts
└── reduced-motion.motion.ts`,
}[framework]
---

  <div class="guide-callout">
    <strong>Copy source, not a package.</strong>
    <p>
      Kida uses the {guide.cliName} CLI to place readable component files directly in your
      project. This is the only way to install Kida today, because the npm packages are not
      published. Copied files are yours to edit and do not update automatically.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="before-you-start">Before you start</h2>
  <p>You need:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>a {guide.project} project;</li>
    <li>Node.js and npm, pnpm, yarn, or Bun;</li>
    <li>a {guide.cliName} <code>components.json</code> file.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Kida's registry installs public runtime dependencies automatically. You do not need
    <code>{guide.packageName}</code>, <code>@kida-ui/motion</code>, or any
    other unpublished Kida package.
  </p>

  <h2 id="prepare-your-project">Prepare your project</h2>

  <h3 id="start-a-new-project">Start a new project</h3>
  <p>Start a new project:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={guide.createCommand} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    {guide.createNote}
  </p>

  <h3 id="use-an-existing-project">Use an existing project</h3>
  <p>From the root of a {guide.compatible} project, initialize its configuration once:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={initCommand} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    If the CLI reports missing Tailwind configuration or an import alias, complete the
    <a href={guide.setupUrl} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
      {guide.setupName}
    </a>
    before continuing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="install-a-component">Install a component</h2>
  <p>Each component page provides its own registry command. For example, install TextBloom:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={installCommand} lang="bash" />
  <p>The command:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>downloads the component from Kida's public registry;</li>
    <li>installs its public runtime dependencies;</li>
    <li>writes the component, styles, and local motion helpers into your project.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    When you add another Kida component, review any overwrite prompt before accepting it if you
    have already edited a shared helper.
  </p>

  <h2 id="use-the-component">Use the component</h2>
  <p>Import the copied source from your project's configured alias:</p>
  <QuickStart name="text-bloom" framework={framework} />
  <p>
    Start your development server and refresh the page to replay viewport-triggered animations.
    Component pages document the available props, variants, accessibility behavior, and live
    examples.
  </p>

  <h2 id="understand-the-copied-files">Understand the copied files</h2>
  <p>A TextBloom installation creates this structure by default:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={structure} lang="text" />
  <p>
    The <code>kida</code> directory namespaces Kida source inside the CLI's conventional
    <code>components/ui</code> directory. Keep the relative structure intact so internal imports
    continue to resolve.
  </p>
  {
    framework === 'svelte' && (
      <p>
        SvelteKit 3 projects import the same files through <code>#lib</code> instead of
        <code>$lib</code>, for example <code>#lib/components/ui/kida/text-bloom.svelte</code>.
        SvelteKit 2 and Vite projects use <code>$lib</code>, as in the examples on this site.
      </p>
    )
  }

  <h2 id="copy-manually">Copy manually</h2>
  <p>
    If you do not want to use the {guide.cliName} CLI, open a component page and expand
    <strong>Copy manually</strong>. Install the listed dependencies, create every file at its shown
    target path, and preserve the directory structure.
  </p>
  <p>
    Manual copying is useful when your project does not use shadcn, but the registry command is
    the safer default because it handles dependencies and configured aliases for you.
  </p>

  <h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</h2>

  <h3>“A components.json file is required”</h3>
  <p>Run <code>{initCommand}</code> from your project root, then retry the Kida command.</p>

  <h3>Tailwind or import-alias validation fails</h3>
  <p>
    Finish your CLI's setup for the selected framework. Confirm that <code>components.json</code> points to
    the same alias your TypeScript and bundler configuration understand.
  </p>

  <h3>The import cannot be resolved</h3>
  <p>
    Check the path created by the CLI. With the default alias, TextBloom is imported from
    <code>{guide.importPath}</code>. Do not import it from an unpublished
    <code>@kida-ui/*</code> package.
  </p>

  <h3>The component renders but does not animate</h3>
  <p>
    Refresh the page, make sure the element enters the viewport, and check whether your operating
    system requests reduced motion. Kida intentionally avoids non-essential motion when
    <code>prefers-reduced-motion: reduce</code> is active.
  </p>

  <h3>The copy button fails</h3>
  <p>
    Allow clipboard access for the site, or select the command directly from the code block and
    copy it with your keyboard.
  </p>

  <h2 id="verify-your-installation">Verify your installation</h2>
  <ol class="verification-list">
    <li>Confirm the expected files exist under <code>{guide.directory}</code>.</li>
    <li>Render the component and check the browser console for errors.</li>
    <li>Try its documented variants or options.</li>
    <li>Run your production build, such as <code>pnpm build</code>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    A successful production build confirms the copied source, imports, types, and runtime
    dependencies work together outside the Kida repository.
  </p>
